    slug: "is-our-data-reliable",
    category: "Data reliability",
    audiences: ["Large companies", "Startups & scale-ups"],
    title: "Which dbt tests should you add first? A data quality starter set",
    standfirst: "Adding a test to every column feels thorough and ends in alert fatigue: hundreds of warnings nobody reads. Start with the tests that protect keys, joins, freshness and the totals the board sees, and give every one that can fail a run an owner.",
    blurb: "The first dbt tests to add, in order: keys, relationships, freshness and a reconciliation test. Plus severity, thresholds and the settings that stop alert fatigue.",
    answer: "Add dbt tests first where a failure would change a decision: unique and not_null on every primary key, relationships on the joins your reports depend on, source freshness on every source, and one reconciliation test that ties a headline total to the ledger. Leave descriptive columns untested or at warn severity, and set thresholds, so the tests that fail a run are few, owned and always acted on.",
    glance: [
      "Test keys and joins first: unique and not_null on primary keys, relationships on foreign keys.",
      "Add source freshness to every source, so stale data fails before anyone reads a report.",
      "One reconciliation test that ties a headline total to the ledger is worth more than fifty column tests.",
      "Use warn severity and warn_if / error_if thresholds for known-dirty columns, so errors stay rare and real.",
      "Every test that can fail a run needs a named owner who decides what the failure means.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["What are the dimensions of data quality?", "Common dimensions are completeness, uniqueness, validity, consistency, timeliness and accuracy. In practice we test six things: completeness, uniqueness, validity, referential integrity, freshness and reconciliation against a trusted total."],
      ["Who should own data quality?", "The business owns the definition of correct; the data team owns the checks and the alerting. Each critical dataset needs a named owner who decides what a failure means and who fixes it."],
      ["What is alert fatigue in data testing?", "Alert fatigue is what happens when tests fail or warn so often that people stop reading them. It usually comes from testing every column at error severity. Fix it by testing fewer, more important things, using warn severity and thresholds for known issues, and giving every error-level test an owner."],
      ["What tools test data quality?", "dbt tests (unique, not null, accepted values, relationships) cover most needs if you already use dbt. Great Expectations and Soda are standalone options. For spreadsheet-based reporting, a checks tab with row counts and control totals is a valid start."],
      ["How often should data quality checks run?", "Every time the data loads. A check that runs only when someone remembers is an investigation, not a control."],
    ],
