    slug: "incremental-models-at-scale",
    category: "Data systems",
    audiences: ["Large companies", "Startups & scale-ups"],
    title: "How to reduce BigQuery and Snowflake costs on billion-row tables",
    standfirst: "At a few million rows, a full rebuild every night is fine. At a few billion, the same model scans years of history to add one day. The levers that fix it are well documented; most teams pull only one of them.",
    blurb: "Incremental strategies (merge, insert_overwrite, delete+insert, microbatch), partition pruning, clustering, late data, and how to estimate cost before you run.",
    answer: "Reduce cost on billion-row tables by making each run read and write only the partitions that changed: an incremental model with the right strategy for your warehouse, a source filter the engine can actually prune on, and a lookback window for late data. Then measure bytes scanned or warehouse time per model before and after, because the most expensive query in most projects is a full scan nobody knows is happening.",
    glance: [
      "Cost follows bytes scanned (BigQuery on-demand) or warehouse running time (Snowflake credits), not row count.",
      "Merge reads the destination as well as the source; partition overwrite and microbatch replace whole time slices instead.",
      "Partition pruning only works when the filter compares the partition column, unwrapped, to a constant or a scripting variable.",
      "Late-arriving data is handled with a lookback window and idempotent batches, not by hoping the pipeline ran in order.",
      "Estimate before you run: a BigQuery dry run reports bytes; on Snowflake, credits are size rate times running time.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["dbt merge vs insert_overwrite: what is the difference?", "Merge matches incoming rows to existing rows on a unique key, updating matches and inserting the rest, so it has to read the destination to find matches. Insert_overwrite on BigQuery replaces whole partitions: every row in the affected dates is deleted and rewritten from the new result. On Snowflake, dbt's insert_overwrite replaces the entire table, not partitions, so it is not a partition strategy there."],
      ["What is dbt microbatch and when should you use it?", "Microbatch (dbt 1.9 and later) splits an incremental model into time batches by an event_time column, such as one batch per day. Each batch is an independent, idempotent query, so you can backfill a date range with --event-time-start and --event-time-end, rerun only failed batches with dbt retry, and reprocess recent batches with the lookback setting."],
      ["Why is my BigQuery query scanning the whole table even with a date filter?", "Usually because the filter cannot prune: the partition column is wrapped in a function BigQuery cannot see through, compared with a subquery result, or combined with another condition using OR. Rewrite the filter so the bare partition column is compared with constant values, and turn on require_partition_filter for large tables so unpruned queries fail instead of billing."],
      ["How to estimate BigQuery or Snowflake query cost before running it", "On BigQuery, a dry run returns the bytes the query will process, and maximum_bytes_billed makes a query fail rather than exceed a limit. Clustering can reduce the real figure below the estimate. On Snowflake, cost is the warehouse size's credit rate multiplied by running time, billed per second with a 60-second minimum each time the warehouse resumes, so test on a representative slice and time it."],
      ["Partitioning vs clustering: which do you need?", "Usually both, for different jobs. Partition on the column your incremental logic and most queries filter by, typically an event or load date, so whole partitions can be skipped and replaced. Cluster on the columns people filter or join on within those dates, such as account or customer ID."],
    ],
    keyword: "reduce Snowflake and BigQuery costs on large tables",
