    slug: "change-data-capture",
    category: "Data reliability",
    audiences: ["Large companies", "Startups & scale-ups"],
    title: "How to make change data capture (CDC) reliable in your data warehouse",
    standfirst: "Replication looks solved until someone counts open orders in both places. Deletes that never arrive, duplicates after a restart, placeholder values overwriting real ones: the failure modes of change data capture are specific, and so are the fixes.",
    blurb: "Log-based vs query-based CDC, deletes and tombstones, ordering and duplicates, unchanged TOAST values, replication slot risk and schema drift, with an idempotent merge.",
    answer: "Make change data capture (CDC) reliable by reading changes from the database log, applying them idempotently so the latest log position wins, and handling deletes explicitly. Then reconcile row counts and keys against production on a schedule, because delivery is at-least-once and query-based syncs miss deletes.",
    glance: [
      "Polling on updated_at misses hard deletes and can miss rows from transactions that commit late.",
      "Log-based CDC reads the write-ahead log or binlog in commit order and sees deletes.",
      "Delivery is at-least-once after failures, so the apply step must be idempotent: latest log position wins.",
      "On Postgres, unchanged TOAST columns can arrive as a placeholder; a blind merge overwrites real values with it.",
      "An unconsumed replication slot retains WAL on the primary. Monitor slot lag like you monitor disk.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["What is log-based change data capture?", "Log-based CDC reads the database's own transaction log (the write-ahead log, or WAL, in PostgreSQL; the binary log in MySQL) and turns each committed insert, update and delete into an event. Unlike polling a timestamp column, it sees deletes and every intermediate change, in commit order, without querying the tables."],
      ["Does Debezium or Kafka give exactly-once delivery?", "Not end to end. Debezium's own FAQ says that when things go wrong, consumers can see events more than once, for example after a connector crash. Treat delivery as at-least-once and make the warehouse apply step idempotent, so replaying an event changes nothing."],
      ["How to handle hard deletes in CDC", "Decide the rule once. Keep a soft-delete flag in the raw layer for audit (Fivetran's _fivetran_deleted, or the delete operation from Debezium) and exclude deleted rows in staging models, so every downstream model inherits one rule rather than each analyst filtering differently."],
      ["How to catch schema changes before they break dbt models", "Add not-null and accepted-values tests on the replicated columns your models depend on, run them on every load, and ask the application team to flag migrations that drop, rename or retype those columns. A rename usually arrives as a new column plus an old one that stops updating, so a not-null test on the old column fails on the first run after the deploy."],
      ["Why is my Postgres replication slot filling the disk?", "In PostgreSQL logical replication, a slot records how far a consumer has read, and the server retains all write-ahead log the slot still needs. If the connector stops or falls behind, WAL accumulates on the primary. PostgreSQL 13 and later can cap this with max_slot_wal_keep_size, at the cost of invalidating the slot and needing a re-sync."],
    ],
