    audiences: ["Large companies", "Startups & scale-ups"],
    title: "SCD type 2 in dbt: snapshots and point-in-time joins explained",
    standfirst: "If your customer table is overwritten in place, every reassignment quietly restates history: last year's regional revenue changes and nobody signed it off. Keeping history is a modelling decision, and the joins that use it have sharp edges.",
    blurb: "Slowly changing dimensions without the jargon: as-was vs as-is reporting, dbt snapshots, valid_from and valid_to, point-in-time joins, backdated changes and the tests that catch gaps.",
    answer: "A type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD) keeps one row per version of a record, with valid_from and valid_to dates, so each sale can join to the version that was true when it happened. In dbt you capture those versions with a snapshot, then use a point-in-time join so last year's revenue stays with the region it was earned in, while a separate current-region field answers 'as is' questions.",
    glance: [
      "Overwriting attributes in place (type 1) silently restates every past report that groups by them.",
      "Type 2 keeps one row per version with a validity interval; facts join to the version valid at the transaction time.",
      "dbt snapshots capture state when they run, so changes between runs are lost and recorded dates are capture dates.",
      "Backdated business changes (a territory move effective last month) need an effective-dated table, not a snapshot.",
      "Test for overlapping intervals, more than one current row, and facts that fall before the first version.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["What is a slowly changing dimension?", "A dimension is a descriptive table such as customers or products. It changes slowly when attributes like region, segment or account owner change occasionally. The SCD types describe how you handle that: type 1 overwrites, type 2 adds a new row per version with validity dates, and type 3 keeps a previous value in an extra column."],
      ["How do dbt snapshots work?", "A dbt snapshot compares the current source rows with the stored history on each run. With the timestamp strategy it uses an updated_at column; with the check strategy it compares listed columns. Changed rows are closed by setting dbt_valid_to and a new version is inserted with dbt_valid_from. Hard deletes can be ignored, invalidated or recorded as a new row."],
      ["Should reports use the region at the time of sale or the current region?", "Both are legitimate and answer different questions. Commission, historical performance and anything already reported to the board usually needs the region at the time of sale. Territory planning usually needs the current region. Expose both as separate, clearly named fields rather than choosing silently."],
      ["Why does revenue drop after joining to a dbt snapshot?", "Almost always because some facts fall outside every validity interval: transactions dated before the customer's first snapshot row, or a gap left by an invalidated delete. An inner join drops them. Use a left join with an explicit rule for the earliest version, and test that totals are the same before and after the join."],
    ],
