    audiences: ["Large companies", "Startups & scale-ups"],
    title: "How to model multi-entity consolidation and FX translation in a data warehouse",
    standfirst: "The group pack is assembled from three ledgers, three currencies and a spreadsheet of intercompany balances that never quite nets to zero. A warehouse can carry most of that mechanically, if it is modelled the way finance actually consolidates.",
    blurb: "Translation at closing, average and historical rates, why YTD at the current average is wrong, intercompany matching and eliminations, versioned rates and constant currency.",
    answer: "Model multi-entity consolidation as data: map every entity's ledger to one group chart of accounts, translate each period's movements at the rate type the account requires (closing for balance sheet, average or transaction rates for profit and loss, historical for equity), and post eliminations to a separate eliminations entity. Keep rates and mappings versioned so any past pack can be reproduced, and reconcile the result to the statutory consolidation rather than replacing it.",
    glance: [
      "Under IAS 21, assets and liabilities translate at the closing rate; income and expenses at transaction rates, with an average rate allowed as an approximation.",
      "Translate monthly movements and add them up. Translating a year-to-date balance at this month's average gives a different, wrong answer.",
      "The translation difference (CTA) is the result of the method. Calculate it; never type it in.",
      "Every intercompany line needs a trading partner, or eliminations cannot be matched.",
      "Version rates and mappings. A restated rate should never silently change a pack the board has already seen.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["Average rate or closing rate for the P&L?", "Under IAS 21, income and expenses are translated at the rates on the transaction dates, and an average rate for the period may be used as an approximation unless rates fluctuate significantly. Balance sheet items use the closing rate. US GAAP (ASC 830) applies the balance sheet date rate to assets and liabilities and the recognition-date rates (a weighted average is generally appropriate) to income and expenses. Agree the rate source and averaging method with your auditors and write it down."],
      ["What is the cumulative translation adjustment?", "It is the difference that arises because the balance sheet is translated at the closing rate while profit and loss and equity use other rates. It is recognised in other comprehensive income and accumulated in equity. In a data model it is calculated as the balancing figure of the translated trial balance, then checked, never keyed by hand."],
      ["Why don't intercompany balances net to zero?", "Usually timing (one side has booked an invoice the other has not), currency (each side translated or revalued at a different rate or date), or mapping (the balance sits in a non-intercompany account on one side). A matching report by entity pair and period shows which, before any elimination is posted."],
      ["Can a data warehouse replace a consolidation tool?", "Rarely for statutory accounts. The warehouse is good at the management view: the same mapping and translation logic applied daily, drillable to transactions and reconciled to the statutory consolidation. Statutory consolidation, with its judgements and audit trail, usually stays in the finance system or a dedicated consolidation tool."],
      ["What is constant-currency growth?", "Growth calculated with both periods translated at the same exchange rates, commonly the prior period's average rates. It separates trading performance from currency movement, and it should be shown alongside reported growth, not instead of it."],
    ],
