    title: "How to calculate MRR from Stripe, and why yours doesn't match",
    standfirst: "MRR summed from invoices, MRR from the Stripe dashboard and MRR in the board deck are three different numbers. Annual plans, prorations, coupons, failed payments and currencies each move them apart. Here is how to define it once and break it into movements a board can read.",
    blurb: "MRR from subscriptions, not invoices: annual plans, proration, coupons, refunds, past_due, cancellations, FX, and a movements model for new, expansion, contraction and churn.",
    answer: "Calculate MRR from Stripe subscriptions, not invoices: take every active and past_due subscription at month end, normalise each price to a month, and exclude taxes, free plans and metered usage, which is how Stripe defines it. In-house figures that sum invoices or payments will not match. Then write one policy for discounts, failed payments and currency, and report each month's change as new, expansion, contraction, churn and reactivation.",
    glance: [
      "MRR is a property of subscriptions at a moment in time, not a sum of invoices in a month.",
      "Normalise intervals: an annual price divided by 12, a quarterly price by 3.",
      "Prorations, refunds and one-off invoice items are cash and revenue events; they rarely change MRR.",
      "past_due still counts in Stripe's MRR; canceled and unpaid count as churn. Choose your policy and write it down.",
      "Movements need a zero-filled customer-by-month table, or churned months disappear from the calculation.",
    ],
    faq: [
      ["How does Stripe calculate MRR?", "Stripe's Billing analytics defines MRR as the sum of the monthly-normalised value of all active and past_due subscriptions, excluding taxes, subscribers on free plans and metered products. A subscription that is canceled or marked unpaid counts as churn. Whether discounts are subtracted is configurable, and permanent recurring discounts are always subtracted."],
      ["How to count annual plans in MRR", "Yes, at one twelfth of the annual recurring price for each month the subscription is active, not as the full invoice in the month it was billed. That is how Stripe normalises intervals, and it is what makes month-on-month movements meaningful."],
      ["Do refunds reduce MRR?", "Not by themselves. A refund is a cash and revenue event. MRR changes when the subscription changes: a cancellation, a downgrade or a new discount. If refunds are frequent because customers cancel and are refunded, the cancellation is what should reduce MRR."],
      ["What is the difference between NRR and GRR?", "Both start from the recurring revenue of customers you had at the start of the period. Net revenue retention adds their expansion and subtracts contraction and churn, so it can exceed 100%. Gross revenue retention subtracts only contraction and churn, so it cannot exceed 100%. New customers are excluded from both."],
      ["Why does my MRR differ slightly from Stripe's report?", "Check time zones first. Stripe's prebuilt Billing reports use UTC, while dashboard charts use your account's time zone, so a cancellation at 11pm on the last day of the month can land in different months. Then check FX and the discount setting."],
    ],
